Main code compiles. Now tests. Write test support: MutableClock, TestConfig (primary MutableClock bean + H2 shutdown on context close), and test classes:

1. `SegmentFlowTest` — full chain via TestRestTemplate:
   - create segment → 201, status REQUESTED
   - invalid time range → 422
   - assign with insufficient qualification → 422
   - assign OK → overlap scenario: 09:00-10:00 assigned; new segment 09:30-10:30 assign same pilot → 409
   - idempotent assign replay: same requestId same params → 200 same assignment id; same requestId different pilot → 409
   - boarding point change before boarding → 200, history kept; after boarding → 409
   - board → board replay same requestId → 200; board different requestId → 409
   - cancel assignment after boarding → 409
   - occupation extends until actual disembark: boarded segment est 09:00-10:00, boarded, new segment 10:00-11:00 → 409 before disembark; disembark at 10:10; new segment 10:05-11:00 → 409; new segment 10:10-11:00 → 200
   - disembark before boarded time → 422
   - cancel assignment before boarding → releases; can re-assign
   - vessel cancel keeps boarding facts: create, assign, board, vessel cancel → timeline still shows boardedAt; assignment released
   - timeline endpoints: pilot timeline, vessel timeline with boarding point history

2. `ConcurrencyTest`:
   - concurrent assign same pilot overlapping segments → exactly one success; failed requestId reusable （失败不占请求键）
   - board vs cancel race → exactly one final state

Use service layer directly for concurrency (easier) plus real H2. Use @SpringBootTest with TestRestTemplate for flow test; concurrency test can @Autowired services.

MutableClock: needs to be a @Primary bean replacing Clock. Test configuration:

```java
@TestConfiguration
static class TestClockConfig {
    @Bean @Primary MutableClock mutableClock() { return new MutableClock(Instant.parse("2026-10-04T08:00:00Z")); }
}
```

But main Clock bean also exists → two Clock beans; @Primary resolves injection. OK.

MutableClock class in test sources:

```java
public class MutableClock extends Clock {
    private final AtomicReference<Instant> instant;
    public MutableClock(Instant initial){...}
    public void setInstant(Instant i)
    getZone/withZone/instant
}
```

Cleanup: @BeforeEach delete from boarding_point_change, assignment, segment. Keep pilots.

H2 release after context: DisposableBean in test config executing "SHUTDOWN". Careful: SHUTDOWN with Hikari — on context close order: DisposableBean of our config runs before datasource close? Bean destruction order isn't strictly guaranteed but depends on dependencies. If our bean depends on DataSource (inject it), it's destroyed before the datasource. Inject JdbcTemplate → depends on DataSource → destroyed first. Good.

Also failed requestId reuse: after a 409 overlap failure with requestId R, use R again for a non-overlapping segment → should succeed (since failure rolled back, no key persisted).

For the flow test, fixed times: use Instant strings; the mutable clock controls boarded_at/created_at. Set clock to 2026-10-04T08:00:00Z initially; for boarding set to 09:05 etc.

Note: board uses Instant.now(clock) for boarded_at. Disembark time from request. Overlap rule for boarded-not-disembarked uses 'infinity' — fine.

One issue: in `assign` catch DuplicateKeyException — Spring's DataSourceTransactionManager: when a DataAccessException is thrown inside @Transactional and caught, transaction continues. OK.

Another subtle issue: `findLatestBySegmentId` after insert filters `a.id() == id` — fine.

Test for "同参重放首次结果" for assign: response 200 with same assignment id.

Let me now write test files. Base dir: src\test\java\com\example\starter.

Test 1 file: SegmentFlowTest.java (~250 lines, may need split into two writes). Let me structure:

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class SegmentFlowTest {
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate rest;
    @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc;
    @Autowired MutableClock clock;

    @BeforeEach cleanup + clock reset

    helpers: createSegment(vessel, level, start, end, point) -> ResponseEntity<SegmentResponse-ish Map>
```

I'll use Map/JsonNode for responses to keep it simple. Use `rest.postForEntity` with parameterised types. Use JsonNode for response bodies.

Actually using records as response types with TestRestTemplate: Jackson needs to deserialize records — fine with Jackson 2.15+. SegmentResponse is a record, deserializable. AssignmentResponse too. I'll use them.

Timeline entries are records containing model records — also deserializable. For timeline GET, use JsonNode to avoid generics hassle.

