```java
@SpringBootTest
class ConcurrencyTest {
    @Autowired AssignmentService assignmentService;
    @Autowired SegmentService segmentService;
    @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc;
    @Autowired MutableClock clock;

    @Test concurrentAssignSamePilotOverlap_onlyOneSucceeds:
        create two segments S1 (09:00-10:00), S2 (09:30-10:30) same required level
        run two threads: assign(S1, P001, "req-c1"), assign(S2, P001, "req-c2")
        CountDownLatch start; ExecutorService; futures with 10s timeout
        count: exactly one success, one ApiException with 409 PILOT_OCCUPIED
        then: failed requestId reused for non-overlapping segment S3 (10:30-11:30) → success （失败不占请求键）
```

Wait: if S1 wins (09:00-10:00), S3 10:30-11:30 no overlap → ok. Reuse the failed requestId.

    @Test boardVsCancelRace_onlyOneEffectiveState:
        repeat 20 times: create segment, assign; then race board(reqId) vs cancelAssignment; assert exactly one succeeds; final state consistent: if board won → assignment boarded, cancel got 409; if cancel won → RELEASED, board got 409.
        Each iteration needs new segment. Use latch to start both simultaneously.

Timeout: future.get(10, SECONDS).

Also a direct H2 repository test? The flow test already exercises real H2 writes/rollback. Rollback verification: after 422/409 failures, assert no assignment row exists and segment status unchanged — do via jdbc query. Good, that covers "回滚在H2上实际验证".

Unique constraint test: duplicate request_id direct insert? The idempotency tests cover it at API level. Could add repository-level test inserting duplicate request_id expecting DuplicateKeyException — good for "唯一约束在H2上实际验证". Add to concurrency test or a small PersistenceTest. I'll add assertions in flow test: after successful assign, jdbc insert duplicate request_id → expect DuplicateKeyException.

Now MutableClock + config. Put MutableClock in test package com.example.starter. Test config as @TestConfiguration imported via @Import or nested in each test class. If nested static @TestConfiguration in one test class, it applies only there. Both test classes need it. Make it a top-level class in test sources annotated @TestConfiguration and @Import it, or make it a @Configuration component-scanned? Test sources are component-scanned by @SpringBootTest since same package — a @Configuration class in test sources under com.example.starter would be picked up by component scan. But @TestConfiguration is excluded from scanning. Simplest: annotate config with @Configuration in test sources → picked up by scan (it's in same package tree). That works but also would apply to all tests — fine.

Careful: defining a @Primary Clock bean of type MutableClock; test autowires MutableClock.

H2 shutdown bean: 

```java
@Bean
DisposableBean h2Shutdown(DataSource ds) {
    return () -> { try (var c = ds.getConnection(); var st = c.createStatement()) { st.execute("SHUTDOWN"); } };
}
```

DisposableBean as @Bean: Spring will call destroy() of the returned DisposableBean? If the bean itself implements DisposableBean, its destroy() is called at context close. Yes.

Order: bean depends on DataSource → destroyed before DataSource. Good.

